ated": {"date-parts": [[2007, 2, 28]], "date-time": "2007-02-28T07:25:31Z", "timestamp": 1172647531000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2361, "type": "book-chapter"}, {"DOI": "10.1201/9781439821916", "type": "monograph", "created": {"date-parts": [[2010, 7, 19]], "date-time": "2010-07-19T14:54:25Z", "timestamp": 1279551265000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2329, "title": ["Handbook of Applied Cryptography"], "author": [{"given": "Alfred J.", "family": "Menezes", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Paul C.", "family": "van Oorschot", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Scott A.", "family": "Vanstone", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2018, 12, 7]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2018, 12, 7]]}}, {"DOI": "10.1103/physrevlett.68.557", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2002, 7, 26]], "date-time": "2002-07-26T21:27:31Z", "timestamp": 1027718851000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2172, "title": ["Quantum cryptography without Bell’s theorem"], "author": [{"given": "Charles H.", "family": "Bennett", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Gilles", "family": "Brassard", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "N. David", "family": "Mermin", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Physical Review Letters"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[1992, 2, 3]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[1992, 2, 3]]}}, {"abstract": "<jats:p>Quantum cryptography is arguably the fastest growing area in quantum information science. Novel theoretical protocols are designed on a regular basis, security proofs are constantly improving, and experiments are gradually moving from proof-of-principle lab demonstrations to in-field implementations and technological prototypes. In this paper, we provide both a general introduction and a state-of-the-art description of the recent advances in the field, both theoretical and experimental. We start by reviewing protocols of quantum key distribution based on discrete variable systems. Next we consider aspects of device independence, satellite challenges, and protocols based on continuous-variable systems. We will then discuss the ultimate limits of point-to-point private communications and how quantum repeaters and networks may overcome these restrictions. Finally, we will discuss some aspects of quantum cryptography beyond standard quantum key distribution, including quantum random number generators and quantum digital signatures.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1364/aop.361502", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2020, 2, 27]], "date-time": "2020-02-27T11:30:07Z", "timestamp": 1582803007000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2059, "title": ["Advances in quantum cryptography"], "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6165-5615", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "S.", "family": "Pirandola", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "U. L.", "family": "Andersen", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "L.", "family": "Banchi", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "M.", "family": "Berta", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "D.", "family": "Bunandar", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "R.", "family": "Colbeck", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "D.", "family": "Englund", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "T.", "family": "Gehring", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "C.", "family": "Lupo", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0032-3999", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "C.", "family": "Ottaviani", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "J. L.", "family": "Pereira", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "M.", "family": "Razavi", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "J.", "family": "Shamsul Shaari", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5410-3329", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "M.", "family": "Tomamichel", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8765-8758", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "V. C.", "family": "Usenko", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4965-5801", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "G.", "family": "Vallone", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "P.", "family": "Villoresi", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "P.", "family": "Wallden", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Advances in Optics and Photonics"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2020, 12, 14]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2020, 12, 14]]}}, {"abstract": "<jats:p>\n                    Our main result is a reduction from worst-case lattice problems such as GapSVP and SIVP to a certain learning problem. This learning problem is a natural extension of the “learning from parity with error” problem to higher moduli. It can also be viewed as the problem of decoding from a random linear code. This, we believe, gives a strong indication that these problems are hard. Our reduction, however, is quantum. Hence, an efficient solution to the learning problem implies a\n                    <jats:italic>quantum</jats:italic>\n                    algorithm for GapSVP and SIVP. A main open question is whether this reduction can be made classical (i.e., nonquantum).\n                  </jats:p>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    We also present a (classical) public-key cryptosystem whose security is based on the hardness of the learning problem. By the main result, its security is also based on the worst-case quantum hardness of GapSVP and SIVP. The new cryptosystem is much more efficient than previous lattice-based cryptosystems: the public key is of size Õ(\n                    <jats:italic>n</jats:italic>\n                    <jats:sup>2</jats:sup>\n                    ) and encrypting a message increases its size by a factor of Õ(\n                    <jats:italic>n</jats:italic>\n                    ) (in previous cryptosystems these values are Õ(\n                    <jats:italic>n</jats:italic>\n                    <jats:sup>4</jats:sup>\n                    ) and Õ(\n                    <jats:italic>n</jats:italic>\n                    <jats:sup>2</jats:sup>\n                    ), respectively). In fact, under the assumption that all parties share a random bit string of length Õ(\n                    <jats:italic>n</jats:italic>\n                    <jats:sup>2</jats:sup>\n                    ), the size of the public key can be reduced to Õ(\n                    <jats:italic>n</jats:italic>\n                    ).\n                  </jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1145/1568318.1568324", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2009, 9, 8]], "date-time": "2009-09-08T08:53:03Z", "timestamp": 1252399983000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 1952, "title": ["On lattices, learning with errors, random linear codes, and cryptography"], "author": [{"given": "Oded", "family": "Regev", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Tel Aviv University, Tel Aviv, Israel"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Journal of the ACM"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2009, 9]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2009, 9]]}}, {"DOI": "10.1145/1060590.1060603", "type": "proceedings-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2005, 8, 3]], "date-time": "2005-08-03T04:31:47Z", "timestamp": 1123043507000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 1721, "title": ["On lattices, learning with errors, random linear codes, and cryptography"], "author": [{"given": "Oded", "family": "Regev", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Tel-Aviv University, Tel-Aviv, Israel"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Proceedings of the thirty-seventh annual ACM symposium on Theory of computing"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2005, 5, 22]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2005, 5, 22]]}}, {"DOI": "10.1109/sp.2016.55", "type": "proceedings-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2016, 8, 18]], "date-time": "2016-08-18T16:34:03Z", "timestamp": 1471538043000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 1629, "title": ["Hawk: The Blockchain Model of Cryptography and Privacy-Preserving Smart Contracts"], "author": [{"given": "Ahmed", "family": "Kosba", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Univ. of Maryland, College Park, MD, USA"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "au